Label the keys in use

The practice's keyboard labelled every key its table placed, the capital
drawn up and to the left of the small letter, so that each key held two
overlapping letters; with a table of characters alone, it labelled the
keys learnt in earlier practices of the same run instead. A practice now
labels the keys that type the characters of its phrase and no other, so
that a child finds them at once: each key once, with the first character
the table gives it unshifted, the small letter a real key shows, drawn in
black, centred on the key, and blended into the keyboard by its own alpha,
pixel by pixel: a plain blit of the label, a surface with alpha, onto the
keyboard, another, turned the letter's soft edges into dark specks and
lines.
